.TH PUTNAMI 1 "" "putnami 0.0.0-golden" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
putnami \- polyglot build orchestrator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B putnami
.RI < command[,command...] >
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B putnami
.RI < structured-command >
.RI < subcommand >
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Putnami is a polyglot build orchestrator for monorepo workspaces.
It reads extension manifests, resolves a dependency DAG, and executes
tasks in parallel via subprocesses.
.PP
Multiple commands can be chained with commas:
.B putnami <command>,<command> --impacted
.SH JOB COMMANDS
No extension is loaded. Name a language extension in putnami.workspace.json, then run: putnami extensions install
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -v " , " --verbose
Show job results and diagnostics
.TP
.BR -d " , " --debug
Stream all events in real-time
.TP
.BR -h " , " --help
Show help
.SH PROJECT SELECTION OPTIONS
.TP
.B --all
All projects
.TP
.B --impacted
Projects affected by git changes
.TP
.B --projects " <list>"
Comma-separated project names
.TP
.B --tag " <tags>"
Filter by tags
.TP
.B --exclude-tag " <tags>"
Exclude by tags
.TP
.B --baseline " <branch>"
Custom git baseline for --impacted; a job command with no target or --all implies --impacted
.SH EXECUTION OPTIONS
.TP
.B --credential-fd " <n>"
Read the run credential from descriptor <n>; keeps it out of every environment and file; custody level 2
.TP
.B --no-cache
Force re-execution (skip cache)
.TP
.B --continue-on-error
Don't abort on first failure
.TP
.B --dry-run
Show changes without applying
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.B --output " <format>"
Output format: text | json | jsonl | cloud-logging
.TP
.B --json
Shorthand for --output=json
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress non-error output
.TP
.B --color
Force color output
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable color output
.SH DEFAULT PROJECT SELECTION
With no project target, feature branches run impacted projects vs trunk.
On main or master, Putnami checks the local same-command/params marker first,
then an active remote-cache successful-run marker, and falls back to all projects
when no usable marker exists.
.TP
.B .
Current directory scope (project or subtree).
.SH STRUCTURED COMMANDS
.SS CI
.TP
.B impact-plan
Emit the impacted plan of a change for a command list.
.SS Projects
.TP
.B projects list
List canonical workspace projects.
.TP
.B projects create
Create a project from template.
.TP
.B projects describe
Show project info.
.TP
.B projects sync
Sync projects from disk.
.TP
.B projects tag
Manage project tags.
.TP
.B scopes list
List workspace scopes (projects, groups, tags).
.SS Workspace
.TP
.B init
Initialize a new workspace.
.TP
.B install
Install extensions and dependencies.
.TP
.B upgrade
Upgrade CLI, extensions, and dependencies.
.TP
.B pin
Pin the CLI version this workspace uses.
.TP
.B workspace describe
Describe the active workspace.
.TP
.B cli list|use
Installed CLI binaries.
.SS Extensions & Templates
.TP
.B extensions install
Install extensions from config.
.TP
.B extensions list
List extensions.
.TP
.B extensions update
Update extensions.
.TP
.B extensions remove
Remove an extension.
.TP
.B extensions prepare
Prepare the provider runtimes of a source workspace.
.TP
.B extensions validate
Validate an extension manifest.
.TP
.B templates install
Install templates from config.
.TP
.B templates list
List installed templates.
.TP
.B templates update
Update templates.
.TP
.B templates remove
Remove a template.
.TP
.B templates validate
Validate a template manifest.
.TP
.B templates test
Test a template in a temporary workspace.
.TP
.B templates package
Package a template into an archive.
.SS Dependencies
.TP
.B deps install
Install project dependencies.
.TP
.B cache clean
Clear this repo's shared build cache.
.TP
.B cache gc
Garbage-collect global caches to their budgets.
.TP
.B cache verify
Verify cache determinism and restore equivalence.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B config show
Show effective configuration.
.TP
.B config set
Set a configuration value.
.TP
.B context generate
Regenerate AI context.
.TP
.B context map
Build or print the workspace orientation map.
.TP
.B context pack
Write or check each project's agent-context document.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B doctor
Preflight production readiness under a deployment profile.
.TP
.B tree fingerprint
Print the digest identifying this worktree's exact state.
.TP
.B sessions list
List recent execution sessions.
.TP
.B sessions inspect
Inspect a specific session.
.TP
.B sessions replay
Resume delivery of a retained execution session.
.TP
.B sessions export
Export recorded sessions as JSONL.
.TP
.B sessions summary
Summarize recorded sessions, one row per run.
.TP
.B report
Read back a run's report.
.SS Migration
.TP
.B migrate vnext
Migrate putnami.lock.json to the current format.
.SS Agents
.TP
.B mcp
Run the MCP server over stdio for AI agent harnesses.
.TP
.B mcp install
Register the MCP server in .mcp.json for Claude Code.
.SS Other
.TP
.B telemetry on|off|status|show
Manage anonymous telemetry.
.TP
.B completion <shell>
Generate shell completions (bash, zsh, fish).
.SH COMMAND REFERENCE
.SS impact-plan
Emit the impacted plan of the exact commit delta from --base to the checked-out HEAD for a comma-separated command list: the changed files, the impacted projects, and every task the engine plans for those commands. An extension reads this ImpactPlan instead of importing the CLI, and derives its own document from it, such as a ChangePlan. The worktree must be clean and --base must be a HEAD ancestor. Source contents, environment values, secrets, command arguments, and output paths are never emitted.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami impact-plan <commands> --base <commit> [--head <commit>] [--no-cache] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --base
Required base commit or ref; emitted as its full immutable commit SHA.
.TP
.B --head
Optional head commit or ref; it must resolve to the checked-out HEAD.
.SS projects list
List every project in the loaded workspace graph with its canonical name, path, type, and tags; use projects describe for dependencies, dependents, extensions, and metadata.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami projects list [--output <format>]
.SS projects create
Create a project from template.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami projects create <name> --template <template> [--path <path>] [--channel <channel>] [--force]
.TP
.B --template
Template to scaffold from.
.TP
.B --path
Workspace-relative project path.
.TP
.B --channel
Resolve the template, the project setup and the dependency install on one release channel (default: PUTNAMI_CHANNEL, then the channel the CLI was installed from, then latest).
.TP
.B --force
Overwrite an existing project directory.
.SS projects describe
Show detailed project information including dependencies, extensions, and configuration.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami projects describe <name> [--output <format>]
.SS projects sync
Sync project references from disk into workspace configuration.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami projects sync [--prune] [--skip-install] [--dry-run]
.TP
.B --prune
Drop config entries whose project directory no longer exists.
.TP
.B --skip-install
Do not run dependency installation after syncing.
.SS projects tag
Add or manage tags on a project.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami projects tag <name> <tag> [--set] [--remove]
.TP
.B --set
Replace the project's tags with the given list.
.TP
.B --remove
Remove the given tags from the project.
.SS scopes list
List workspace scopes with their projects, groups, and tags.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami scopes list [--output <format>]
.SS install
Run workspace-level installation: extensions, templates (if configured), and dependencies.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami install [--latest]
.TP
.B --latest
Ignore the lock file and resolve the latest extension and template versions.
.SS upgrade
Upgrade the CLI, extensions, templates, and framework dependencies in one command; a channel resolves on each ecosystem's own registry.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami upgrade [--global] [--cli] [--extensions] [--deps] [--channel <stable|canary>] [--release <rs_id> --namespace <namespace>] [--version <version>] [--from-source] [--dry-run]
.TP
.B --global
Upgrade the global CLI in ~/.putnami/bin instead of the workspace pin; inside a workspace the other phases still run, outside one only the CLI is upgraded (alias: -g).
.TP
.B --cli
Only upgrade the CLI binary.
.TP
.B --extensions
Only upgrade extensions and templates.
.TP
.B --deps
Only upgrade framework dependencies.
.TP
.B --channel
Resolve a release channel (stable by default; canary for latest prerelease).
.TP
.B --release
Use one immutable release-set snapshot for npm and Go dependencies (needs --namespace).
.TP
.B --namespace
Release-set namespace that published the --release id; accepted only with --release.
.TP
.B --version
Use an exact Putnami release version.
.TP
.B --from-source
Build the CLI from this workspace's source and install it as the active binary, bypassing the download channel (self-host escape hatch; combine with --global to replace the global CLI).
.TP
.B --dry-run
Resolve and print the upgrade plan without modifying files.
.SS pin
Pin the putnami CLI version this workspace uses, recording its per-platform digest in putnami.lock.json so `putnami` resolves and verifies the exact binary.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami pin [<version>] [--remove]
.TP
.B --remove
Remove the CLI version pin.
.SS workspace init
Initialize a new workspace.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami init [--workspace <name>] [--extension <ts|go|py>] [--project <name>] [--project-path <path>] [--channel <channel>] [--force]
.TP
.B --workspace
Workspace name override.
.TP
.B --extension
Default extension to install.
.TP
.B --project
Create an initial project.
.TP
.B --project-path
Workspace-relative path for the initial project.
.TP
.B --channel
Resolve the extensions, the template and the starter dependencies on one release channel (default: PUTNAMI_CHANNEL, then the channel the CLI was installed from, then latest).
.TP
.B --force
Reinitialize an existing workspace.
.SS workspace describe
Confirm the active workspace and show its name, version, project count, extension count, and configuration summary before selecting work.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami workspace describe [--output <format>]
.SS cli
List the installed putnami CLI binaries (list) and switch the active one (use).
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cli <list|use>
.SS cli list
List the installed putnami CLI binaries in .putnami/bin (or ~/.putnami/bin with --global) and mark the active one.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cli list [--global]
.TP
.B --global
List binaries in ~/.putnami/bin instead of the workspace (alias: -g).
.SS cli use
Switch the active putnami CLI binary to an installed putnami-<name> (e.g. one built by `upgrade --from-source`).
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cli use <name> [--global]
.TP
.B --global
Switch the binary in ~/.putnami/bin instead of the workspace (alias: -g).
.SS extensions install
Install extensions from workspace configuration and run extension install hooks.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions install [name] [--latest] [--user] [--platform <os/arch>] [--dest <dir>] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --latest
Ignore the lock file and resolve the latest versions.
.TP
.B --user
Pin one registry extension in the user scope (~/.putnami/user) so its commands run outside any workspace.
.TP
.B --platform
Materialize artifacts for another platform (skips install hooks and the lock write).
.TP
.B --dest
Materialize into this artifact-store root instead of the machine-global one.
.SS extensions list
List declared and discovered extensions with their versions and source.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions list [--user] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --user
List the extensions pinned in the user scope (~/.putnami/user) instead of the workspace.
.SS extensions update
Update extensions to latest compatible versions.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions update [name] [--output <format>]
.SS extensions remove
Remove an installed extension.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions remove <name> [--user]
.TP
.B --user
Remove the extension from the user scope (~/.putnami/user) instead of the workspace.
.SS extensions prepare
Prepare into the artifact store the runtime of each path extension that serves a provider in a workspace that builds its own CLI. A hosted run of the same tree starts these runtimes and prepares none itself. ./putnamiw --print-engine runs this command after it builds the CLI.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions prepare [--output <format>]
.SS extensions validate
Develop extensions: validate manifests against the protocol.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami extensions validate [path]
.SS templates install
Install templates from workspace configuration.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates install [name] [--latest] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --latest
Ignore the lock file and resolve the latest versions.
.SS templates list
List configured and discovered templates.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates list [--output <format>]
.SS templates update
Update templates to latest compatible versions.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates update [name] [--output <format>]
.SS templates remove
Remove an installed template.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates remove <name>
.SS templates validate
Validate a template manifest against the protocol.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates validate [path]
.SS templates test
Test a template by rendering it, then creating a project from it in a temporary workspace and running the build and test its extension declares.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates test [path] [--skip-build] [--keep] [--channel <channel>]
.TP
.B --skip-build
Render the template without running build and test on it.
.TP
.B --keep
Keep the rendered temporary workspace for inspection.
.TP
.B --channel
Install the template's extension and create the project on one release channel (default: PUTNAMI_CHANNEL, then the channel the CLI was installed from, then latest).
.SS templates package
Package a template into a distributable archive.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami templates package [path] [--version <version>] [--stable]
.TP
.B --version
Version to stamp on the produced archive.
.TP
.B --stable
Publish under a stable version instead of a prerelease.
.SS deps install
Install project dependencies by delegating to extension-provided installers.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami deps install [--tag <tags>] [--exclude-tag <tags>]
.SS cache clean
Delete cached build artifacts from this repo's shared store (affects all worktrees of the repo); --all clears every repo's store.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cache clean [--all]
.SS cache gc
Garbage-collect the machine-global build, Go, Bun, and binary stores down to their byte budgets.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cache gc
.SS cache verify
Run the workspace gate tasks (the blocking commands of putnami.ci.json; lint, test and build without one) twice in isolated worktrees, compare cache keys and captured artifacts, restore the first run into a pristine tree, and fail on undeclared writes or live-versus-hit differences; declared ambient key inputs are reported without failing.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami cache verify [--projects <selector> | --impacted | --all] [--output <format>]
.SS config show
Display the fully merged configuration (global + workspace + local scopes).
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami config show [--output <format>]
.SS config set
Set a configuration value in the workspace config.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami config set <key> <value>
.SS context generate
Regenerate the Putnami guidance block in CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md, and the agent skills the workspace declares by in-tree path. Bytes outside the block are never touched; an edited block is preserved and reported, and --force rewrites that block only. No other file is written: run `putnami mcp install` to register the MCP server.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami context generate [--force]
.TP
.B --force
Rewrite a guidance block that was edited or written by an older release.
.SS context map
Regenerate the ephemeral workspace map under .putnami/context-map/ (repo-map.json and repo-map.md) from committed artifacts (project manifests, schema/openapi.json, schema/config.jsonschema.json, READMEs, and the workspace docs tree). Byte-deterministic on an unchanged tree, gitignored, and never committed. --print renders it to stdout and writes nothing. A build writes no map. Agents get the same document fresh from the MCP workspace_map tool.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami context map [--print[=json|md]] [--projects <list>] [--impacted] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --print
Render the map to stdout (md by default, or json) and write nothing.
.SS context pack
Write one deterministic agent-context document per project to <project>/.gen/agent-context.json: its identity and graph, composition roots, commands, config and contracts. --check re-derives every document, writes nothing, and fails when one drifted. The MCP agent_context tool builds the same document in memory and reports whether the file is fresh.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami context pack [--project <selector>] [--check] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --project
Project ID, path, or name; default is every workspace project.
.TP
.B --check
Re-derive the documents, write nothing, and fail on drift.
.SS doctor
Run a read-only production-readiness preflight over the selected projects: derives production-safety findings (incomplete capabilities, missing required config, invalid committed schemas, config shadowing) from committed manifests and grades them by deployment profile. Under --profile production a high/critical finding fails with exit 2; dev/test stay advisory. Config values are never read or emitted.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami doctor [--profile <dev|test|production>] [--project <selector>] [--machine] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --profile
Deployment profile to evaluate under (dev|test|production); production is strictest.
.TP
.B --project
Project ID, path, or name (comma-separated); default is every workspace project.
.TP
.B --machine
Report the one-minute load per logical CPU, or unknown, instead of the readiness preflight; takes neither --profile nor --project.
.SS tree fingerprint
Print one digest covering HEAD, the tracked diff against it, and the content of every untracked non-ignored file. It exists because "the same files are dirty" is not "the same bytes are on disk": git status reports paths and status codes, so an agent that gates a tree and then edits a file it had already dirtied leaves that output identical. Human output is the bare digest; --output=json adds dirty and headSHA, the same members a recorded session's tree block carries. Reads the repository and writes nothing, and needs no workspace.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami tree fingerprint [--output <format>]
.SS sessions list
Show recent execution sessions with timing and status. --revision keeps the sessions whose recorded tree sat on the named commit (a lowercase hex prefix of its id, 7 to 64 characters) and adds the head commit and the actual placement to each row, so a new session can find what judged a given revision; a session that recorded no tree never matches. --output=jsonl rows carry revision and placement when the record states them.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami sessions list [--revision <sha>] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --revision
Keep only sessions whose recorded tree sat on this commit; a lowercase hex prefix of the commit id, never a ref name.
.SS sessions inspect
Show full details and event stream for a specific session. --run names a remote attempt instead of a session: the attempt reference or submission key a remote run printed when it was submitted, resolved through the durable attempt records under .putnami/runner/attempts. An attempt whose session is already imported is shown from the local store; one that is not (the CLI died mid-run, the import failed) is resumed through the workspace's runner provider from the persisted cursor and imported through the same path, and nothing is ever resubmitted. Ctrl-C during a resumed follow cancels the attempt within bounded time.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami sessions inspect [<id>] [--run <ref>] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --run
Inspect the session of a remote attempt by its attempt reference or submission key, resuming the attempt first when it was never imported.
.SS sessions replay
Replay retained session artifacts through each explicitly selected reporter extension (session-reporter, log-reporter) that has not delivered them. Each resumes from its own acknowledgement cursor with fresh reporter credentials, without running graph work. Incomplete delivery returns an error and keeps the bounded checkpoint for another replay.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami sessions replay --session <id>
.TP
.B --session
Exact retained session id; latest is not accepted.
.SS sessions export
Stream every session document still present under .putnami/sessions, one per line, oldest first, deduplicated by session id. Each line is the recorded document forwarded verbatim (only whitespace is folded), so every line declares its own protocolVersion and a record written by a pre-v2 CLI keeps the version-1 shape. A session with no session.json, and a document that does not parse, are skipped rather than failing the command. The store is pruned and a worktree is disposable, so this is how per-run CPU, task counts and cache reuse leave a worktree before it disappears.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami sessions export [--since <timestamp>] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --since
Keep only records whose recorded start time is at or after this RFC 3339 timestamp; a record with no readable start time is not emitted.
.SS sessions summary
Reduce every session this worktree recorded to one row: session id, start time, commands, selection, task total/executed/reused, run.cpu.actualMs, wall and outcome. It shares `sessions export`'s ordering and deduplication, so a store reached through a worktree symlink still counts each run once. A run a task spawned — a validation guard that builds what it checks is the canonical case — carries the session that spawned it and is marked nested, so it is never mistaken for a second gate. --command keeps TOP-LEVEL sessions whose recorded command set is EXACTLY the one named, order-insensitive: a nested run never matches whatever it ran, so a guard that ran the full gate cannot be counted as a second gate. --by-digest regroups the selected sessions' task records by their input digest — the cache key each task was keyed on — with how many records carry it and how many executed, reused or failed, so a reader tells why a task ran without re-running it. With --output=json the rows travel as the shared result envelope's data array at every count — an empty result is an empty array, and a single result is not a bare object.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami sessions summary [--since <timestamp>] [--command <list>] [--by-digest] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --since
Keep only records whose recorded start time is at or after this RFC 3339 timestamp; a record with no readable start time is not listed.
.TP
.B --command
Keep only top-level sessions whose recorded command set is exactly this comma-separated list, in any order; a nested run never matches.
.TP
.B --by-digest
Group the selected sessions' task records by input digest: records per digest, and how many executed, reused or failed.
.SS report
Print the bounded synthesis a finished run recorded under .putnami/reports: the newest CLI-driven run's by default, or the one named by --session. With --output=json the recorded document is printed as-is, unwrapped, because it is the contract a consumer binds to. An interrupted run records no report, so a consumer that files a report against a commit verifies its git.sha rather than trusting recency.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami report [--session <id>] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --session
Report to read, by session ID (default: the newest CLI-driven run's).
.SS migrate vnext
Check or atomically migrate putnami.lock.json to the current format while preserving pins and recording any missing installed extension task-contract metadata.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami migrate vnext [--check] [--apply] [--output <format>]
.TP
.B --check
Report the pending migration without writing (default); exits non-zero when a migration is needed.
.TP
.B --apply
Perform the migration, atomically and idempotently.
.SS mcp
Run the Model Context Protocol server over stdio so an AI agent harness can discover projects, context, ownership, impact, plans, and diagnostics through 11 typed core tools (list_projects, describe_project, agent_context, workspace_map, deps, find_owner, why_impacted, topo_sort, impacted, run_jobs, get_diagnostics) and the workspace://context resource. All except run_jobs are read-only; run_jobs is the only mutating core tool, supports dry-run planning, and refuses long-lived or externally mutating execution. Spawned by the client; speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 on stdin/stdout and exits with the session. Before it serves, it brings the workspace's agent workflows to the version the lock pins, from the local artifact store only, and reports what it cannot reconcile as a warning on stderr. Optional request provenance is disabled by default; set PUTNAMI_AGENT_IDENTITY=1 and explicitly set PUTNAMI_AGENT_MODEL in the MCP server environment to propagate bounded harness/model headers.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami mcp [install]
.SS mcp install
Write the putnami entry into .mcp.json at the workspace root, where Claude Code discovers the MCP server. Codex, Cursor and VS Code read their own files and do not see this entry. init, install and upgrade already add a missing entry; this explicit write is the only one that also rewrites a diverged putnami entry. Merge-aware: other servers and unknown keys are preserved, and an unparseable file is refused untouched.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami mcp install
.SS telemetry on
Enable anonymous usage telemetry.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami telemetry on
.SS telemetry off
Disable anonymous usage telemetry.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami telemetry off
.SS telemetry status
Check current telemetry settings.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami telemetry status
.SS telemetry show
View buffered telemetry events pending flush.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami telemetry show [--output <format>]
.SS completion
Generate a shell completion script for bash, zsh, or fish; source it or install it where the shell looks for completions.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami completion <bash|zsh|fish>
.SS help
Show help for putnami, for one command, or for the whole CLI in man or Markdown format.
.PP
.B Usage:
.br
putnami help [<command>] [<subcommand>] [--man] [--markdown]
.TP
.B --man
Render the full reference as a man page.
.TP
.B --markdown
Render the full reference as Markdown.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: a job/build/test failed, or an unexpected internal error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: bad flags or arguments, an unknown command, no projects/jobs matched, or a contract/config validation error.
.TP
.B 3
Auth: authentication or authorization failed.
.TP
.B 4
API: a remote or upstream API call failed.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by signal (Ctrl+C).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_OUTPUT
Default output format (text, json, jsonl, cloud-logging).
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_VERBOSE
Enable verbose output.
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_QUIET
Suppress non-error output.
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_CACHE_TRUST
Remote cache trust policy (ci, any, or none). The resolved value is also exported to every job.
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_CACHE_OBJECT_SOCKET
Set BY the CLI for each job: the cache provider's object-cache socket. Absent under --no-cache, under trust none, or without a provider.
.TP
.B PUTNAMI_NO_COLOR
Disable color output.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Standard no-color convention.
